    $astro3_master$> We have read the *daily* sky (the shadow of Earth's spin) and the *yearly* sky (the shadow of Earth's tilt). Now the sky's third great clock — the *monthly* one — and the object that keeps it: the Moon. The Moon is the most dramatic and changeable thing overhead, cycling through its **phases** — new, crescent, quarter, gibbous, full, and back — over about a month, a rhythm so reliable it gave us the very word "month." And once again the appearance hides a simple geometry, and once again there is a tempting wrong explanation to clear away. The phases are *not*, as many believe, the Earth's shadow falling on the Moon — that would be a lunar eclipse, a rare event, not the monthly cycle. They have nothing to do with any shadow. They are the shadow, in this course's deeper sense, of one beautifully simple fact: **the Moon is a sphere, the Sun lights exactly half of it at all times, and as the Moon orbits we view that permanently half-lit ball from continuously changing angles — so we see a changing *fraction* of the lit half.** Let us build that geometry, and read the whole monthly Moon out of it.

Watch the Moon for a month and you see something no other object does: it *changes shape.* The stars are fixed, the Sun an unvarying disk, the planets steady dots — but the Moon transforms, night by night, from a thin crescent low in the west after sunset, through a half-disk, a bulging gibbous, to a brilliant full Moon rising in the east as the Sun sets, then thinning back through gibbous and crescent until it vanishes entirely at new Moon, and begins again.

{{image: Lunar phase | The Moon through a lunar cycle — new, crescent, quarter, gibbous, full, and back. The Moon itself is not changing; what changes is how much of its permanently sunlit half turns toward Earth as it orbits. The word "month" descends from "moon," so ancient was this clock.}}

Ask people what causes this and a great many — including, surveys find, a large fraction of well-educated adults — give an answer that sounds reasonable and is completely wrong: *the phases are the shadow of the Earth falling on the Moon.* It is a natural guess, but here is the first clue it fails: you can often see a crescent Moon and the Sun in the sky at the same time. If the Moon's dark part were Earth's shadow, the Earth would have to sit *between* the Sun and the Moon to cast it — and then the two could never appear together. The fact that we routinely see the Sun and a partly-lit Moon in the same sky *proves* the darkness is not Earth's shadow. And a second clue: the phases cycle smoothly *every* month, like clockwork, while the Earth passes directly between Sun and Moon only occasionally — and when it does, we get a **lunar eclipse**, a distinct, rarer event (a topic for the next chapter). The phases and the eclipse-shadow are two different things. So the phases are not a shadow at all, but something more elegant: the changing view of a ball that is always half-lit.

## A half-lit sphere, seen from a moving point

The whole thing rests on two fixed facts and one changing quantity. **Fact one:** the Moon is always exactly half-lit. It is a sphere of rock that shines only by reflecting sunlight, and the Sun, far off to one side, illuminates whatever hemisphere faces it — a permanent day side and a permanent night side, at every moment, regardless of phase. The lighting of the Moon never changes. **Fact two:** the Moon orbits the Earth, about once a month, so its position relative to the Sun–Earth line continuously shifts. Put the two together and the phases fall out: as the Moon orbits, we view its unchanging lit hemisphere from continuously changing angles, and how much of the lit half we can see depends entirely on where the Moon sits in its orbit. Walk it around:

```moonphase
```

When the Moon lies between us and the Sun, its lit half faces away and we see darkness — the **new** Moon. A quarter-turn later the Sun–Moon–Earth angle is 90° and we catch the lit half edge-on, a **half-disk** — a *quarter* Moon (named for being a quarter of the way through the cycle, not for showing a quarter of a disk). Opposite the Sun, the lit face turns fully toward us — the **full** Moon. In between we see in-between fractions: a **crescent** (a sliver of the lit side, between new and quarter) or a **gibbous** Moon (most of it, between quarter and full). As the lit fraction grows we call the Moon **waxing**; as it shrinks, **waning.** And the lit portion always bulges toward the Sun, because that is where the light comes from — which is why a crescent's bright edge and horns reveal the Sun's hidden position below the horizon. Notice, in the picture above, that there is no shadow anywhere: the Earth casts none on the Moon, yet every phase appears. The phases are geometry-of-viewing-angle, not geometry-of-shadow — another case where the real arrangement overturns the plausible-but-wrong first guess. (The Earth's shadow *does* reach the Moon, but only at certain full Moons, giving a lunar eclipse — a separate story.)

## Two kinds of month

A subtle and lovely consequence trips up almost everyone: the phase cycle is *longer* than the Moon's actual orbit. The **sidereal month** — the Moon's true orbital period, one lap relative to the stars — is about 27.3 days. The **synodic month** — the phase cycle, new Moon to new Moon — is about 29.5 days. Why is the phase cycle two days longer than the orbit? Because a phase depends on the Moon's angle to the *Sun*, not to the stars, and while the Moon orbits the Earth, the Earth carries it about a thirteenth of the way around the Sun, so the Sun's direction shifts. After one full orbit the Moon has returned to the same place against the stars, but the Sun has "moved on," and the Moon must travel about two extra days to catch up and line up with the Sun again. Put numbers to the beat:

```example
synodic-month
```

The lunar month you actually watch is a *combination* of two motions — the Moon around the Earth and the Earth around the Sun — so the Moon's month carries the fingerprint of the Earth's year. (Lunar calendars count the synodic month, which is why their months run 29 or 30 days and drift against the solar year.)

## Why we always see the same face

In all of human history, until spacecraft went there, no one ever saw the far side of the Moon: the same familiar face, with the same dark seas and bright highlands, has looked down on everyone who ever lived. Why? The answer, **tidal locking**, contains a subtlety that fools many people. From "we always see one face," many conclude the Moon does not rotate. The truth is the opposite: the Moon keeps one face toward us *because* it rotates — exactly once per orbit. If it did not rotate at all, then as it went around it would present every side to us in turn; to keep the *same* face inward it must turn on its axis once for each orbit, its spin keeping perfect pace with its revolution. Its rotation period (27.3 days) *equals* its orbital period (27.3 days), and that equality is what locks one hemisphere toward us. Walk in a circle around a chair while always facing it: to do so you must turn your own body once per lap — an outside observer sees your front, side, back, side — yet the chair only ever sees your face. The Moon does exactly this around the Earth.

{{image: Far side of the Moon | The far side of the Moon, first photographed by the Soviet Luna 3 in 1959. It is not the "dark side" — it gets just as much sunlight as the near side, and is fully lit at new Moon — merely the side that never faces Earth. It looks strikingly different: heavily cratered, with almost none of the dark volcanic plains that form the "man in the Moon."}}

Why are the two periods exactly equal? Not by coincidence, but by tidal locking. Earth's gravity raises a slight bulge on the Moon; long ago the Moon spun faster than it orbited, which carried that bulge ahead of the Earth–Moon line, and Earth's pull on the leading bulge created a braking torque that slowed the spin — until it matched the orbit, the bulge fell into line, the torque vanished, and the rotation locked. Given billions of years, tidal braking reliably produces this synchronous rotation, which is why most large moons in the solar system are tidally locked to their planets. And note the common misnomer it dispels: the far side is not the "dark side." It receives just as much sunlight as the near side — indeed it is *fully* lit at new Moon, when the near side is dark to us. It is *far*, not dark.

## The phase is a clock

Here is a genuinely useful payoff. Because a Moon's phase *is* its angle from the Sun, the phase fixes when the Moon rises, culminates, and sets — so you can read the time of night from the Moon, and the phase from the Moon's place. Try it:

```moonclock
```

The new Moon shares the Sun's direction, so it rises at dawn and hides in the daytime glare. The first quarter trails the Sun by a quarter-turn — rising at noon, highest at sunset, ruling the first half of the night. The full Moon lies opposite the Sun, rising exactly as the Sun sets and staying up all night (which is why "the Moon rises as the Sun sets" is specifically a *full*-Moon thing). The last quarter rises near midnight and haunts the predawn. So a crescent low in the west after sunset must be young and waxing; a Moon high at midnight must be full; a half-Moon in the morning sky must be last quarter — the whole orbit, legible at a glance. And one more elegant detail: the Moon rises about **fifty minutes later each day**, the direct signature of its own orbital motion. Watch it march:

```example
moonrise-delay
```

## The Moon as our first ruler to the cosmos

There is a historical payoff worth its own moment, because it connects this chapter to the deepest theme of the whole programme — that the sky gives us directions but not distances. The Moon was humanity's *first stepping-stone* to measuring the cosmos, the first world beyond Earth whose real size and distance we worked out, using nothing but the phase-and-eclipse geometry of this chapter. Around 270 BC, **Aristarchus of Samos** realised a lunar eclipse is a measuring opportunity: watching the curved edge of Earth's shadow sweep across the Moon, he compared their sizes and found the Moon about a quarter of Earth's diameter — essentially correct. Knowing the Moon's true size and measuring its angular size in the sky (about half a degree) then gave its distance — about thirty Earth-diameters, again close to the truth. Then he reached for the Sun, using the phase geometry itself: at a *half* Moon the Sun–Moon–Earth angle at the Moon is exactly 90°, so Earth, Moon, and Sun form a right triangle, and measuring the sky-angle between Moon and Sun at that instant gives the ratio of their distances. Run his reckoning:

```example
sun-distance
```

His measurement was far off — the true angle is agonisingly close to 90°, too close for the naked eye — but the *method* was flawless, and even his underestimate proved the Sun is much farther, and so much larger, than the Moon: far larger, indeed, than the Earth. From that single deduction Aristarchus drew a revolutionary conclusion — that the tiny Earth might orbit the giant Sun — proposing a Sun-centred cosmos eighteen centuries before Copernicus. This is the very first rung of what becomes the great cosmic distance ladder, and it begins here, with someone watching the Earth's shadow cross the Moon and reasoning. When the first chapter said the sky gives directions but not distances, and that recovering the distances would be one of astronomy's great quests — *this is where that quest begins.*

## Three lovely details

Three refinements reward the careful watcher. The first is **earthshine**: look at a thin crescent and you may see the whole disk faintly, the dark part glowing with a soft ashen light — sunlight reflected off the Earth. When the Moon is a thin crescent to us, an astronaut on it would see a nearly *full* Earth blazing in their sky, and that earthlight softly lifts the Moon's night side into view. It is a two-way mirror of the phases — new Moon to us is full Earth to the Moon — first explained, purely from the geometry of reflected light, by Leonardo da Vinci.

{{image: Earthshine | Earthshine — "the old Moon in the new Moon's arms." The bright crescent is direct sunlight; the whole dark disk glows faintly with sunlight reflected off the Earth onto the Moon's night side. When the Moon is a thin crescent to us, the Earth is nearly full in the Moon's sky, casting the most earthlight of all.}}

The second is **libration**: we said we always see the same face, but not *quite* — over time we glimpse about 59% of the surface, because the Moon appears to rock slightly (its orbital speed varies while its spin stays steady) and nod a little, letting us peek around its edges and over its poles. And the third connects the Moon's present to its deep past: the Moon is slowly **receding**, drifting away about 3.8 centimetres a year (measured by bouncing lasers off reflectors the Apollo astronauts left behind) as tides transfer momentum from Earth's spin to the Moon's orbit — lengthening our day and enlarging the Moon's orbit over the eons. The Moon we see is a snapshot of a slowly evolving system: locked to us in rotation, drifting from us in distance.

## The shadow of a lit sphere

Gather the argument, because this chapter completes our reading of the sky's three great clocks — daily, yearly, monthly. We began with the changing Moon and cleared away the shadow misconception — impossible, since we see the Sun and a partly-lit Moon together, and the phases cycle every month while Earth's shadow reaches the Moon only rarely. We built the true geometry: a permanently half-lit sphere, viewed from a moving point, showing us a changing fraction of its lit half — none (new), edge-on (quarter), all (full). We distinguished the sidereal month (the true orbit) from the longer synodic month (the phase cycle), whose extra two days encode the Earth's own motion around the Sun. We solved the same-face puzzle with tidal locking — the Moon rotates *once per orbit*, braked into step by Earth's tides — and corrected the "dark side" to the far side, fully and regularly sunlit. We made the geometry practical, reading the Moon as a clock, and traced the first rung of the distance ladder back to Aristarchus reasoning from this very geometry.

Here is the lesson to carry. The Moon's phases — the most watched, most calendar-defining of all sky phenomena — are, like everything in this course, the shadow of a geometry: not a literal shadow, but a simple three-dimensional arrangement — a lit sphere orbiting the Earth, seen from a moving vantage — whose changing appearance *is* the phases. The daily sky is the shadow of Earth's spin; the yearly sky, the shadow of Earth's tilt; the monthly sky, the shadow of a lit sphere orbiting in space. Three rhythms, three geometries, one skill: reading the geometry from the appearance. And next, we watch what happens on the rare occasions when the geometry lines up *exactly*.

**Next**, eclipses — the precise alignments of Sun, Earth, and Moon that we have carefully set apart from the phases. The Moon's shadow sweeping across the Earth, and the Earth's shadow swallowing the Moon; why eclipses do *not* happen every month; and why a total solar eclipse is among the most extraordinary coincidences, and spectacles, in all of nature.

## Further reading

Any introductory treatment of the lunar phases and the Moon's orbit will serve — but best of all, go outside and watch. Note the Moon's phase and where it sits relative to the Sun over a week or two: the crescent near the Sun at dusk, the full Moon rising opposite the setting Sun a fortnight later. Nothing on the page teaches the phase geometry like seeing it play out overhead.$astro3_master$,
    true,
    true
  )
